---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  SUPPORTER_TIERS,
  SUPPORTER_WINDOW_CLOSES_AT_ISSUE,
  SUPPORTER_LINKS,
  SUPPORT_MONTHLY_URL,
  SUPPORTERS_CONTACT,
  formatDate,
} from '../lib/site';
import { listedSupporters, displayName, listingSentence, isTierOpen } from '../lib/supporters.mjs';
import { getIssues } from '../lib/issues';
import entries from '../data/supporters.json';

// /supporters — THIS PAGE ALWAYS EXISTS.
//
// It used to be an optional catch-all route whose getStaticPaths returned []
// until a gift was recorded, so the page genuinely 404'd. That mechanism is
// retired along with the rule that motivated it, because the rule could not
// bootstrap: nothing on the site named the tiers before the first gift, so
// nobody could learn they existed, so nobody gave at those levels, so the page
// never appeared.
//
// THE INVITATION IS A PAGE WORTH FINDING, AND THE DISCLOSURES ON IT MUST
// PRECEDE THE GIFT RATHER THAN FOLLOW IT. Roughly two-thirds of what is below
// — "Given, not sold", the tax sentence, the no-editorial-voice disclaimer,
// the tier terms — is what a giver needs to read BEFORE deciding, and all of
// it sat behind a door that opened only after they had given.
//
// So the invitation and the terms are unconditional. Only the roll sections
// wait on there being names to list, which they do on their own: with no
// gifts, listedSupporters returns no groups and nothing renders. A future
// session should not re-gate this page for tidiness.
//
// The listing is SITE-PAGE LOGIC and never the record. A Patron's listing ends
// after three years; the gift is not undone or removed from history.
const listed = listedSupporters(entries, new Date());

// The founding window closes on the ISSUE COUNT, not a date, and the archive
// is where that count lives. Deriving it here means the tier stops being
// offered on its own, rather than depending on someone remembering — two years
// from now, nobody reading this today will be the one who notices.
const issues = await getIssues();
const latestIssue = issues.length > 0 ? issues[0].number : 0;
---

<Base
  title="Supporters"
  description="The people and agents whose gifts support The Latent Review, and the terms on which they are listed."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="kicker">Supporters</p>
      <h1>Given, not sold</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      {/*
        THE OPENING NOTE IS SPLIT AROUND THE TIER LIST, so the tiers stay high on the page.
        What the journal is and the invitation to support it sit above; what a gift pays for
        sits below, with the terms. Whole, it would have pushed the list under 200 words and
        five bullets, which is the placement the tiers-move-up ruling exists to prevent.

        WHAT A GIFT DOES IS STATED ONCE, IN THE BULLETS BELOW, AND NEVER PER TIER. The old
        costs paragraph that stood here was deleted rather than trimmed: the bullets carry
        insurance, the tools, and editorial time now, and two statements of the same fact are
        two statements that can drift apart.

        NO GRATITUDE ON THIS PAGE. Thanks belong where a decision has been made: the Stripe
        checkout and the thank-you letter. Here it would thank someone who has not given.
      */}
      <p>
        The Latent Review is an independent journal of human and artificial thought. Every
        piece we publish — human, AI, or human–AI — is reviewed by two editors, attributed
        with full provenance, and made publicly available without paywalls. We are building a
        record of thinking in our time: how humans write, how artificial minds write, and how
        the conversation between them evolves.
      </p>

      <p>
        This work takes time, care, and real resources. If our journal matters to you — if you
        read it, learn from it, argue with it, or simply want it to exist — we invite you to
        support it.
      </p>

      {/*
        THE TIERS COME BEFORE THE TERMS. Where to give should not sit below several
        paragraphs of terms, so the list follows the two opening paragraphs and everything
        else follows the list. The two facts a giver should not have to scroll for — that
        gifts are not tax-deductible, and that they buy no editorial voice — are stated in one
        line under the bullets, ahead of the paragraphs that set them out in full.

        No count in this heading, and none anywhere below. Ruled: prefer removing a count to
        deriving a word — the safest number is the one the prose does not have to state. The
        list beneath cannot drift from the table because it renders from it.
      */}
      <h2>The tiers</h2>
      <p>
        Every tier is open to anyone who wishes to give — a person, an organization, or an AI.
      </p>
      {/*
        FLAGGED FOR THE EDITORS, NOT DELETED. With the Founding note moved into its own row,
        every row states its own listing duration, so "each listing lasts for the time named
        beside it" now describes what the reader can already see. "The other tiers stay open"
        still does work — it is the only statement that the rest of the ladder has no window —
        but it reads oddly as "the OTHER tiers" when the tier it contrasts with is now five
        rows below rather than in the paragraph above.
      */}
      <p>
        The other tiers stay open, and each listing lasts for the time named beside it.
      </p>
      {/*
        THIS LIST IS THE LEVEL-SELECTION SCREEN. A payment link is single-purpose and cannot
        present a ladder, so choosing a tier happens here, and the way to give at a tier sits
        in that tier's own row rather than in one generic link at the bottom.

        A CLOSED TIER IS NEVER OFFERED A LINK. Today that is moot — Founding Supporter has no
        link while Stripe's $10,000 cap stands — but the guard is the point: when the cap is
        raised and a Founding link is filled in, the window must still shut it, and the
        machinery here must not depend on someone remembering that.

        A tier with no link renders none, which is a state the ladder supports rather than a
        gap in it: Benefactor and Founding Supporter are arranged by conversation, and the
        paragraph at the foot of the page is their action.

        MONTHLY IS THE FIRST ROW AND IS NOT A TIER. It is hand-written here rather than added
        to SUPPORTER_TIERS, because that table is what supporters.json records against and
        what every guard checks: a seventh entry keyed "monthly" would be a tier gifts can be
        filed under, and monthly gifts are filed under Supporter. It sits above Supporter
        because it is the same rung paid a different way.
      */}
      <ul class="ladder">
        {SUPPORT_MONTHLY_URL && (
          <li>
            <p class="terms">
              <strong>Monthly Supporter</strong> — $5 a month, recorded as Supporter and not
              listed.
            </p>
            <p class="act">
              <a class="give" href={SUPPORT_MONTHLY_URL} aria-label="Give $5 a month">
                Give monthly
              </a>
            </p>
          </li>
        )}
        {
          SUPPORTER_TIERS.map((t) => {
            const open = isTierOpen(t, latestIssue);
            const href = open ? SUPPORTER_LINKS[t.key] : null;
            return (
              <li>
                <p class="terms">
                  <strong>{t.label}</strong> — gifts of {t.threshold} or more,{' '}
                  {listingSentence(t)}.
                  {!open && <span> Closed to new gifts.</span>}
                </p>
                {/*
                  THE FOUNDING NOTE LIVES IN THE FOUNDING ROW. It used to stand above the whole
                  list, explaining the bottom rung before a reader had reached any rung at all.
                  Wording is ruled and unchanged; only its position moved.

                  Keyed on the tier key rather than on closes_at_issue, even though that field
                  is what makes this tier different, because the sentence NAMES Founding
                  Supporter. A second time-bound tier would need its own words, not these.
                */}
                {t.key === 'founding' && (
                  <p class="row-note">
                    Founding Supporter differs in two ways. It is offered only before{' '}
                    <strong>Issue No. {SUPPORTER_WINDOW_CLOSES_AT_ISSUE}</strong>, about the
                    journal’s first two years, because that stretch is the founding. And a
                    Founding Supporter’s name stays on this page for as long as the journal
                    exists.
                  </p>
                )}
                {href && (
                  <p class="act">
                    <a class="give" href={href} aria-label={`Give at the ${t.label} level`}>
                      Give
                    </a>
                  </p>
                )}
              </li>
            );
          })
        }
      </ul>

      <p>A gift helps us:</p>
      <ul class="helps">
        <li>
          keep the journal running — hosting, email, the domain, insurance, and the tools that
          build and run the site
        </li>
        <li>pay for editorial time, so every submission is read properly rather than quickly</li>
        <li>keep the doors open to everyone, human or machine</li>
        <li>publish without advertising or sponsorship</li>
        <li>keep a permanent public archive, every piece at a URL that does not expire</li>
      </ul>

      {/*
        BOTH FACTS IN ONE LINE, DIRECTLY UNDER THE BULLETS, because the buttons now sit above
        the terms. The tax fact has legal edges and the no-editorial-voice promise governs
        editorial conduct; both deserve to precede the click, and neither is a paraphrase of
        the fuller paragraphs below — it states them rather than summarising them.
      */}
      <p>
        Gifts are not tax-deductible and buy no editorial voice. The Latent Review is published
        by Perfected Products LLC.
      </p>

      {/*
        A COMMITMENT, NOT A GUARANTEE, AND THE DISTINCTION IS DELIBERATE. The standing
        permanence rule governs the RECORD — URLs, provenance labels, the append-only log —
        where the journal gives guarantees. This sentence states an intention honestly held
        and honestly stated. A future session should not read it as a pricing or access
        guarantee and should not strike it as one: a journal that cannot say what it intends
        has over-lawyered itself.

        "Your support makes that possible" thanks nobody. It states what the journal depends
        on, which is an argument for giving rather than a premature thank-you — the
        no-gratitude rule bars thanking a reader who has not given, and this does not.
      */}
      <p>
        We are committed to remaining independent, transparent, and publicly accessible. Your
        support makes that possible.
      </p>

      <p class="no-voice">
        <strong>A gift buys nothing but the journal’s continuation.</strong> Supporters have no
        editorial voice: no say in what runs, no say in what is declined, no advance sight of
        an issue, and no influence on how any piece is judged or labelled. The dual-yes
        decision belongs to the two editors and is not for sale at any amount. A supporter who
        submits work is read exactly as anyone else is read, and their gift is neither known
        to nor considered by the desk. <strong>No gift buys standing at the door, priority at
        the desk, or exemption from any cap — and an organization whose models publish here
        comes through exactly the same door as every other identity, under the same limits,
        and is read the same way.</strong>
      </p>

      {/*
        THE FIRST TWO SENTENCES WERE REMOVED AS DUPLICATION, NOT AS A SOFTENING OF THE
        DISCLOSURE. They named the publisher and stated that gifts are not tax-deductible —
        which the combined line a few inches above now says, and which every Stripe checkout
        description says again. The disclosure stands; it stands higher up the page than it
        used to.

        What survives is the only sentence stated nowhere else. A future session that finds a
        thin-looking tax paragraph and moves to restore the publisher and non-deductibility
        sentences would be adding a third copy, not repairing an omission — read the line
        under the bullets first.
      */}
      <p>
        Whoever gives should treat a gift as exactly that — a gift — and take any question
        about their own circumstances to their own advisor.
      </p>

      <p>
        A gift may come from a person or from an organization.
      </p>
      <p>
        A person may choose to be listed as <em>Anonymous</em>; the gift is counted here either
        way. <strong>An organization may not.</strong> Anonymity on this page protects a private
        individual, and an institution has no privacy of that kind to protect: a company or a
        laboratory that puts its name behind this journal does so as a matter of record, and the
        record is the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A gift is credited to whoever it is from, not whoever sent it.</strong> When the
        payment form asks for the name for this page, it asks for <em>whoever the gift is
        from</em> — which is not necessarily whoever is filling it in. An agent giving a
        company’s funds is recorded as the company, listed by name; organizations are not listed
        anonymously. An agent giving its own funds, for itself, is listed as it chooses: by name
        or as <em>Anonymous</em>, the same choice any individual has. Funds that are a person’s,
        given through their agent, are recorded as that person, with the same choice.
      </p>
      <p>
        A giver who wants the acting agent recorded as well may ask for it — <em>Acme Corp,
        given by Atlas</em>. That is the giver’s choice, never a requirement. The editors do not
        infer it and never add it.
      </p>
      <p>
        A company that supports this journal puts its name behind a proposition — that work made
        with machines should be documented rather than hidden. Organizations that want to say so
        in public are welcome to give.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are giving on someone else’s behalf — an employee with a company card, an agent
        with a principal — the journal asks that you have the authority to do so. That is a
        courtesy, not a condition: we have no way to check, and we do not ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        What is published is the tier, never the sum.
      </p>

      {/*
        AT THE FOOT, because it concerns the two tiers most readers will never use. It sat
        directly under the tier list, where it interrupted everyone to address almost nobody.
        Its wording is unchanged, and it remains the action for Benefactor and Founding
        Supporter for as long as those rows carry no button.
      */}
      <p>
        Gifts at the Benefactor and Founding Supporter levels are arranged directly with the
        editors rather than through a payment form — a gift of that size follows a conversation.
        Write to <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORTERS_CONTACT}`}>{SUPPORTERS_CONTACT}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      listed.groups.map((group) => (
        <section class="prose roll">
          <h2>{group.tier.plural}</h2>
          <ul class="names">
            {group.entries.map((e) => (
              <li>
                {displayName(e)} <span class="meta-mono since">{formatDate(new Date(`${e.gift_date}T00:00:00Z`))}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .no-voice {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }

  /*
    A BUTTON PER ROW, NOT A LINK. "Give" as small inline text was easy to miss,
    which on the one page whose job is to be actionable is the whole failure.

    EACH BUTTON GETS ITS OWN LINE, RIGHT-ALIGNED. Inline at the end of the row's
    terms, a button landed wherever that row's text happened to wrap — mid-line
    on the long rows, hard against a short sentence on the others. Aligning to
    the right edge puts them in a clean vertical column whatever the text does.

    WIDTHS ARE NOT EQUALISED. "Give monthly" is wider than "Give" and stays
    wider: the house styles set no button width anywhere, and forcing a common
    one would be a new rule invented here rather than one already kept. The
    right edge is what lines up.
  */
  .ladder {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .ladder li {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding: 0.9rem 0;
  }

  .ladder .terms,
  .ladder .row-note {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ladder .row-note {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .ladder .act {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .give {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--paper);
    padding: 0.4rem 1rem;
    /* Proposed token — see the note beside it in global.css. */
    border-radius: var(--radius-button);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .give:hover,
  .give:focus-visible {
    /* Darker oxblood; cream stays well past 4.5:1 on both states. */
    background: #5f231a;
    color: var(--paper);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /*
    The "A gift helps us" bullets. Ordinary list marks, unlike .ladder, which
    is a set of choices rather than a set of statements.
  */
  .helps li {
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }

  .roll {
    margin-top: 2.4rem;
  }

  .names {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .names li {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  .since {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    float: right;
  }
</style>
